#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""palivane-posture — device posture reporter: IDE extensions + MCP configs.

The CI scan endpoints vet extension lists and MCP configs point-in-time; this script
turns that into continuous *device* posture without an endpoint agent. It collects what
is actually installed/configured on this machine and reports it through the existing
scan APIs, so drift (a new rogue extension, a new unapproved MCP server) surfaces as a
finding shortly after it happens:

- installed VS Code extensions (`code --list-extensions`) → POST /api/scan/ide-extensions
- AI coding-assistant settings (Cursor / Claude Code) → POST /api/scan/agent-config, to
  flag unsafe autonomy (YOLO / auto-apply / auto-run / --dangerously-skip-permissions)
- MCP server configs → POST /api/scan/mcp-config, from: ~/.claude.json (Claude Code —
  a minimal {"mcpServers": …} view is synthesized; the raw file holds unrelated user
  state and is never posted), ./.mcp.json, VS Code, Cursor, and Copilot CLI user configs.
- agent instruction/rules files → POST /api/scan/agent-rules, to catch hidden-instruction
  injection (the "rules-file backdoor"): CLAUDE.md / .cursorrules / .cursor/rules/*.mdc /
  AGENTS.md / .github/copilot-instructions.md / GEMINI.md / skill SKILL.md files.

Credentials are stripped before anything is posted. This runs on every developer machine
at session start, and an MCP config's `env` block is exactly where a live token sits — it
used to be shipped verbatim so the backend could scan it. Detection now happens here
(palivane_detect, shared with palivane-secrets and the at-rest scanners): each secret is
replaced by «redacted:label» in the content, and reported separately as a category, a
label, and a masked preview. The vetting the backend does — which servers are declared,
what they launch, whether they are allowlisted, what a rules file instructs — never needed
the value.

Runs are cheap and idempotent: a sha256 cache (~/.palivane/posture-cache.json) skips the
POST when nothing changed, so repeated runs don't spam findings. palivane-connect installs
a SessionStart hook that fires `palivane-posture --async --quiet` whenever a Claude Code
session starts; a cron/launchd job works too. Fails open: errors never block anything.

    palivane-posture [--force] [--dry-run] [--quiet] [--async]

Config: PALIVANE_URL + PALIVANE_TOKEN (an ak_… key) from the environment, else from the
`env` block of ~/.claude/settings.json (written by palivane-connect). Stdlib only.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import urllib.request
from datetime import datetime, timezone

